────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  SUMMARY
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  We are testing whether a redesigned structure-aware search algorithm (v3) can find >90% of the true Pareto-optimal configurations in BLIS's 6-knob space within 200 evaluations, up from v2's 57.9% recall, and whether it generalizes across different request arrival rates (50, 100, 200). The v3 algorithm fixes two identified failure modes: non-deterministic Phase 1 coverage that missed critical tiers, and uniform Phase 2 budget allocation that wasted evaluations on tiers with no metric diversity.

  * h-main: v3 search runs at rate=100 across 3 seeds (42, 123, 999). Confirmation requires mean Pareto tuple recall >0.90 and minimum >0.85 (vs. v2 mean=0.579, random baseline=0.312). Three key changes: deterministic Phase 1 (5 fixed configs per GPU tier, no shuffling), diversity-directed Phase 2 (budget proportional to observed ttft_p99 range per tier, focusing on partial-preemption zone kv=2000–4000 at bs=32), and neighbor-expansion Phase 3.
  * h-robustness: v3 search runs at rate=50 and rate=200 (seed=42) against exhaustive baselines at those rates. Confirmation requires tuple recall >0.80 at both rates. This tests whether the algorithm's data-driven tier classification (rich vs. simple based on observed metric variance) adapts to different cache pressure levels without hardcoded rate-specific tuning.
  * Exhaustive baselines at all three rates provide ground-truth Pareto fronts (~1800 configs each, ~396s per rate). Total experiment wall time is under 30 minutes. Refutation signals: if recall stays below 0.85 at rate=100, the structural hypothesis (diversity lives in partial-preemption zone of low-instance tiers) is incomplete; if robustness fails (<0.80 at off-nominal rates), the tier classification threshold (100ms ttft range) needs rate-adaptive calibration.

